<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liébana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liébana is a mountain district in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, enclosed by the Picos de Europa, with Potes as its main town. In the 8th century its monastery of Santo Toribio was home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatus-of-liebana">Beatus of Liébana</a>, author of a famous commentary on the Apocalypse. The monastery keeps what is claimed to be the largest surviving fragment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liebana.jpg" alt="Liébana" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HiTe modificación del original de Emilio Gómez Fernández · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comarca_de_Li%C3%A9bana_(Cantabria)_Mapa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 9th and early 10th centuries the Leonese kings granted the lands of Saldaña, Carrión and <b>Liébana</b> on the upper Carrión river to Count Diego Muñoz, founder of the family later called the Banu Gómez in Arabic sources.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-saldana">County of Saldaña</a></li></ul></section>
